use of 3 tier system architecture 
This architecture separates the system into three distinct layers to ensure it is scalable and easy to maintain, which is a key goal of the "Design phase" of the SDLC.

Tier 1: Presentation Layer (Frontend)
This is the user interface where the three primary user roles interact with the system:

Student Portal: For browsing courses and enrolling.

Lecturer Interface: For managing course details and viewing student lists.

Admin Dashboard: For system oversight and user management.

Tier 2: Application Layer (Backend/Logic)
This "brain" of the system processes the business rules. For your specific project, it should includes:
Authentication Module: Validates logins for all user types.
Enrollment Engine: Manages the logic for students registering for courses.
Course Management Service: Allows lecturers to update course data.
Report Generator: Allows admins to view academic records and system efficiency.

Tier 3: Data Layer (Database)
This is the static structure of your system's data. It should stores:
User Tables: Credentials and profiles for students, lecturers, and admins.
Academic Tables: Course catalogs, department info, and credit hours.
Transaction Tables: Enrollment records linking students to specific courses.

2. Mapping Architecture to Your UML Requirements
To fulfill Phase 4 of your assignment, your team will need to translate this architecture into the following diagrams:
Class Diagram (Static Structure): Use this to design the 8-12 key entities (like Student, Course, Enrollment) and their relationships.
Sequence Diagram (Interaction): Use this to show the message flow between the UI, the Application Logic, and the Database for a specific user story, such as "Student logs in".
Activity Diagram (Process Flow): Use this to model a complex workflow, such as the step-by-step process of "Course Enrollment".
Use Case Diagram (System Boundaries): Define the system boundaries and show how the different actors (Student, Lecturer, Admin) interact with major functions.

3. Proposed Technology Stack

Frontend: HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript (React or Vue.js).

Backend: Node.js, Python (Django), or Java (Spring).


Database: Relational Database Management System (RDBMS) like MySQL or PostgreSQL, as these are best for managing the structured relationships between students and courses
